{-
   `addressOf` of something that is not storage - `resolve_mir/intrinsic/expand_pointer.cpp`.

   A scalar lives in a register, which is the point of it, so taking the address of one is the
   operation that *makes storage exist*. `resolve/Global.yana` states the same rule for a constant
   global - "the address of a temporary holding its value, which is the only reading available for a
   value that cannot be assigned to" - and this is that rule for the two positions inside a body
   where nothing named the value: a literal, and a call result.

   Both stages materialize, and they had better agree about it, which is why this is in the shared
   corpus rather than beside the ownership fixtures. The shipping stage's `materialize` has done it
   since raw pointers existed; the rewrite reached `addAddressOf` with an owned value instead and
   asserted, because an address is the address of somewhere and a node result is nowhere. `lib/Native/Pointer.yana` writes
   `fn addressOf(it: a) -> %a`, so *every* call of it materializes; written `it: source'a` only these
   two would. The answer is the same either way, which is what this fixture is for.

   **What the answers are for.** None of this is visible in the emitted text: an address that was
   the address of a copy, or two temporaries folded onto one slot, both produce well-formed IR and a
   different number.
-}

import Native

fn seven() -> I32 = 7

-- A literal occupies no storage of its own, so what the pointer names is a frame slot the call
-- wrote the value into - and reading it back through the pointer is what says the slot is real.
fn ofLiteral() -> I32 = *addressOf(5 :: I32)

-- A call result is the same case: nothing named it, so nothing gave it a place.
fn ofCall() -> I32 = *addressOf(seven())

-- Two of them are two slots. A materialization that reused one temporary for the whole body would
-- pass every test above and fail this one, which is the reason it is here.
fn distinct() -> I32:
    let p = addressOf(5 :: I32)
    let q = addressOf(5 :: I32)

    if (bitcast(p) :: Size) == (bitcast(q) :: Size):
        return 0

    return 100

-- And a name that does have storage keeps it: nothing is materialized, so a write through the
-- pointer is seen by the next read of the name. An address of a copy answers 20 here.
fn ofStorage() -> I32:
    let &v = 20 :: I32
    let p = addressOf(v)

    store(p, *p + 1)
    return v

fn main() -> I32 = ofLiteral() + ofCall() + distinct() + ofStorage()
